# Internationalisation (i18n)

## Overview

This project uses HAMR's built-in i18n system. Translation files live in `locales/*.json`,
and type-safe Go accessors are generated into `internal/locale/locale.go`.

## Adding a New Language

1. Copy `locales/en.json` to `locales/fr.json` (or your target language code)
2. Translate the values (keep the keys identical)
3. Run `hamr locale gen` to validate and regenerate accessors

## Translation Keys

Keys use dot-separated paths matching the JSON structure:

```json
{
  "home": {
    "title": "Home",
    "welcome": "Welcome to {{.Name}}"
  }
}
```

This produces `home.title` and `home.welcome` keys. The generated accessor for
`home.welcome` will require a `name string` parameter.

## Plurals

Use CLDR plural category keys (`zero`, `one`, `two`, `few`, `many`, `other`):

```json
{
  "items": {
    "one": "{{"{{"}}".Count{{"}}"}} item",
    "other": "{{"{{"}}".Count{{"}}"}} items"
  }
}
```

The generated accessor will take `count int` as the first parameter.
`Count` is automatically injected into the template data.

## Key Naming Rules

- Keys starting with a digit (e.g. `2fa.title`, `404.error`) are valid but the
  generated Go method name will be prefixed with `X` to form a valid identifier
  (e.g. `X2faTitle`, `X404Error`).

## Build-time Validation

`hamr locale gen` (and `make build` / `make test`) validates all locale files
against the default locale. **Interpolation mismatches are hard errors** — if
`en.json` uses `{{"{{"}}.Name{{"}}"}}` but `fr.json` uses `{{"{{"}}.FirstName{{"}}"}}` for the same key,
the build will fail. Missing keys are warnings only (they fall back to the
default locale at runtime).

## Regenerating Accessors

```bash
hamr locale gen
# or
go generate ./internal/locale/...
```

The `hamr dev` watcher also runs this automatically when locale files change.

## Using Translations in Handlers

```go
import (
    "github.com/FyrmForge/hamr/pkg/i18n"
)

func (h *Handler) Index(c echo.Context) error {
    tr := i18n.FromContext(c)
    title := tr.T("home.title")
    // ...
}
```

## Using Translations in Templates

```
templ Page(c echo.Context) {
    <h1>{ i18n.FromContext(c).T("home.title") }</h1>
}
```

## Middleware

- `LocaleFromPath` — extracts locale from URL path prefix (`/en/about`)
- `LocaleFromPreference` — resolves from cookie/Accept-Language/user pref

Region-tagged locales (e.g. `fr-FR`) are automatically resolved to the base
language (`fr`) if the exact tag isn't loaded. This applies to all resolution
sources: user preference function, cookies, and Accept-Language headers.

## RTL Support

The layout template uses `lang` and `dir` attributes:
```html
<html lang={ middleware.GetLocale(c) } dir={ middleware.GetDirection(c) }>
```

Arabic, Hebrew, Farsi, and other RTL languages are detected automatically.
Override via `_meta.direction` in the locale JSON file.
